WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.194 --> 00:00:02.360
DUSTIN INGRAM: I'm Dustin
Ingram, Developer Advocate

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.992
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:03.992 --> 00:00:05.450
Today I'm going to
show you how you

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:08.980
can run a Wagtail application
on Google App Engine.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.570
Wagtail is an open-source
content management system

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.190
built on Django.

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:14.930
It's used by thousands
of organizations

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.280
worldwide with a
strong community.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.900
In fact, Wagtail on App Engine
is what powers the Google Cloud

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.440
Blog at cloud.google.com/blog.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.470
It also looks great, is
focused on user experience,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:31.730
and offers precise control for
both designers and developers.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.950
App Engine makes it easy
to deploy your Django

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:35.150
app to the cloud.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.310
And since Wagtail is
built with Django,

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:40.710
it's easy to get up and
running with Wagtail as well.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.860
Also, similar to
the Django project,

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:45.620
Wagtail recently announced it
has dropped support for Python

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.560
2, which makes it a great
fit for the Python 3

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:51.800
Runtime on the App Engine
standard environment.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.110
A content management
system consists primarily

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.580
of three parts--

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.950
an admin interface for
creating and updating

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:01.880
your content, a database
for storing your content,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.060
and a frontend for
rendering your content.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.970
Wagtail comes with its own
admin interface out of the box.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.150
So the first thing we
need to do to get started

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:14.080
is create a new database
for our project.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.820
Wagtail works with a number
of different databases.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.740
We'll choose Postgres.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.960
On the Google Cloud
Platform console,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.180
we'll go to the Cloud
SQL Instances page.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.360
Here, we'll create
a new instance

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.850
and select Postgres 9.6.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:31.070
We'll just use a development
database for now,

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:33.290
but once your app starts
getting lots of traffic,

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:36.230
you'll probably want to use a
production database instead.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:41.070
Next, we'll specify an instance
ID and default password.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.840
Once the instance is
initialized, we'll select it.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.410
Nice.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.580
Ready to go.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.640
Select the Copy button next
to Instance connection name

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.350
and hang onto this for later.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.720
This string is what we'll
use to tell our app how

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.090
to connect to our database.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.850
Now, we'll go local.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.560
Being able to run
your app locally

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.509
makes it easier to
test changes as you're

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.900
working, without needing
to redeploy every time.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.066
In order to run our
Wagtail app locally,

00:02:15.066 --> 00:02:17.190
we'll need a way to connect
to the database we just

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:19.650
created from our local machine.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.660
We could connect
directly to it, but this

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.550
would require that
we allow our database

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.410
to accept incoming
connections directly

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.170
from our local IP address, which
would make it slightly less

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:29.580
secure.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.730
Instead, we'll use a tool
called Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.720
This is a utility that can run
on our development machine that

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.850
creates a secure tunnel between
our machine and the Cloud SQL

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.690
database.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.830
Then we could configure our
app to talk to this local proxy

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:48.130
instead when running
in development mode.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.561
Installing the Cloud
SQL Proxy is easy.

00:02:50.561 --> 00:02:52.810
See the link in the video
description for instructions

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.940
for your platform.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.970
We'll start the Cloud SQL
Proxy with this command.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.490
You'll use the instance name
you copied in the previous step

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.072
here.

00:03:02.072 --> 00:03:04.030
We'll leave this running
in a separate terminal

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.830
while we continue.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.920
Now in a new terminal, we'll
install the Wagtail package

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.371
locally.

00:03:10.371 --> 00:03:11.870
I'm already in a
virtual environment

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.579
that I created for this project.

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:15.620
If you're not familiar
with virtual environments,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.180
see the link in the
video description

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.040
for guides for setting
up and activating

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.450
a virtual environment.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.460
I highly recommend
that any Python project

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:24.920
starts with its own
virtual environment.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.340
Installing the Wagtail package
gives us the Wagtail command,

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.910
which we can use to generate a
cookie cutter Wagtail project.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.890
We'll call ours mysite.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.650
So we'll use the command
Wagtail start mysite.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.130
This will create a directory
called mysite where

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.172
our Wagtail app will live.

00:03:42.172 --> 00:03:43.880
Let's go into that
directory and see what

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.100
our starter app looks like.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.650
There's a few things in here
that might look familiar.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.040
Most of the core of our
app lives in mysite.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.380
The search and home
directories are the subpages

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.680
that we get by default. We need
to update our Wagtail's app

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.690
settings so that it has
the right information

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.650
to interact with our database.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:07.070
The development settings go in a
file at mysite/settings/dev.py.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:10.100
We'll tell it how to interact
with our Cloud SQL Proxy, which

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.140
should still be
running, by specifying

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.450
local host as our host.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.050
Now we need to do the same
for our production settings.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.630
Ideally, any secret
like a database password

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.640
would be encrypted
with an encryption key

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.530
from Cloud Key
Management Service

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.070
and held in cloud storage, only
to be decrypted at runtime.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:30.320
But we'll just include our
password in the source code

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.970
here to simplify things.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.130
But be sure to read the
guide to secret management

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.140
in the video description.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.450
Similarly, we'll
set a secret key

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.790
which will be used for
cryptographic signing

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:43.100
to secure our tokens,
cookies, et cetera,

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.180
by just assigning
it in the source.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.720
However, in production, this
should be stored as a secret

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.050
as well.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:51.080
We'll also need to
tell our app which host

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:53.060
names it should respond to.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.880
App Engine gives you an
appspot.com domain by default,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.640
so we'll use that here.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.830
Next, we're going to
migrate our database

00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:03.230
and start up our app locally.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.930
Since we opted to use
Postgres as our database,

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.840
we'll need to add the
psychopg2 Python package

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.720
to the requirements.text file
at the root of our project's

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.700
mysite directory.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:18.270
Then let's install everything.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:21.240
We'll run the migrate command
to get our database initialized.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.210
Finally, we'll create
an administrator account

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:30.970
that we'll use to login
to our Wagtail app.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:34.000
Running Python manage.py
createsuperuser

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.190
will prompt us for our
username and password

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.850
for our admin Wagtail user.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:42.150
Now we can run Python
manage.py runserver

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.490
to start the server locally.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.770
The Wagtail tail app will
be visible on local hosts

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:50.340
at port 8000 by default. Let's
check it out in our browser

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:57.420
by visiting
http://127.0.0.1:8000.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.820
Once there, we should see
this generic welcome message.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.870
Now that we have the app
configured, let's deploy it.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:06.510
There's three things we
need to do before deploying.

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:08.820
First, we need to collect
all our static assets,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.070
like CSS and JavaScript
into a single directory.

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:15.150
The collectstatic command
will do this for us.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:18.040
Next we need to
create a main.py file.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.670
This is where App Engine looks
for an app variable which

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.580
should be your application.

00:06:22.580 --> 00:06:25.890
Wagtail puts this elsewhere and
calls it application instead,

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:28.080
so we just need to
connect the dots.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.620
Finally, we need to create an
app.yaml file to hold our App

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:33.420
Engine configuration.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.090
We'll tell it to use
the Python 3 Runtime,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.370
and we'll setup handlers
for our static assets

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.140
and for our other routes.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.510
We'll also tell it to use
our production settings once

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.310
deployed.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.230
When we're done,
our mysite directory

00:06:46.230 --> 00:06:48.580
should look something like this.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.260
Now all we need to do is
deploy with gcloud app deploy.

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:01.650
Once the deployment
is finished, we

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.430
can go visit our app at
its URL on appspot.com,

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.580
and we'll see that same
familiar welcome screen.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.920
We can also sign in with
the super user account

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.000
that we created before.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:17.830
Looking great.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:19.660
We've got a content
management system.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:22.000
Now all that's left to
do is add the content,

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.215
which is easy with
Wagtail's web interface.

00:07:24.215 --> 00:07:25.840
From here, you might
also want to start

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.970
working on your app's
frontend to create a more

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:30.040
engaging experience
for your visitors,

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:33.880
or add some models to begin
defining your business logic.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:36.860
I've shown you how to set
up a Cloud SQL database,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:40.030
create a new Wagtail project,
and deploy it to App Engine's

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:41.880
new Python 3 Runtime.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.380
And I'm confident that you'll
be able to take this and build

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.660
something great on Google Cloud.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.700
The best part about
App Engine is that it

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:50.410
grows with your application.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:53.000
When you aren't getting
traffic, you don't pay anything.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:55.900
And as your app becomes more
popular and traffic increases,

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.990
it scales automatically
to meet that demand.

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:00.700
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:02.200
I'll show you how
to use Stackdriver

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.330
to monitor your Python 3 app.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.372
Feel free to ask questions
in the comments below.

00:08:06.372 --> 00:08:07.829
And if you liked
this video, please

00:08:07.829 --> 00:08:09.880
subscribe to the Google
Cloud Platform channel

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:11.567
for more videos like this.

00:08:11.567 --> 00:08:12.400
Thanks for watching.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

